e coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5130e557-dbd1-5ef5-a8c7-d4385a492641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327639} {:answer/id #uuid "82f57cb5-6e3a-591d-b251-18c5fa9e9ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328061} {:answer/id #uuid "c78abad5-00e2-5685-adbf-cc569b236611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328687} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7487ae-747e-5496-b225-a93e75695f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329140} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9df9ca-a753-5c98-90a1-1814458095ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc9df9ca-a753-5c98-90a1-1814458095ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329150} :answer-field/id #uuid "66615078-b8c1-5283-bff6-9b4e6b55fe7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9c9be89-da09-5bc3-a22f-a4017d770581" :question/math-academy-id "q-42412" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c336cc3-c89e-5af2-b170-1fc6d9cf8307" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring a Number"}] :question/id #uuid "b5313725-78fc-57ee-9acc-19d03f6624bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-13752" :question/problem "Find the value of $4$ raised to the second power." :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate $4^2.$\n\nTo evaluate $4^2,$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2}=\\underbrace{4\\times 4}_{\\text{[math]2[/math] times}}=16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc1a0e6d-ed51-5797-8ab7-305e861d1109" :knowledge-point/title "Cubing a Number"}] :question/id #uuid "213a088d-3427-5313-adc2-1fcc9d64bb74" :question/math-academy-id "e-13754" :question/problem "What is the value of $5$ raised to the power of $3?$" :question/worked-solution "Here, $5$ raised to the power of $3$ means $5^3.$\n\nTo compute this power, we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n![](images/e7/e7beecad2379c3be9452eb111496ed8c65a5d4f747874658167169bba6ff11c9.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $5 \\times 5 = 25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{5\\times 5}_{25}\\times 5 & =25\\times 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out ${\\color{blue}25} \\times 5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{2}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5^3=125.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70a364f5-5384-5ba6-8142-5740aa51f4bc" :knowledge-point/title "Raising Zero or One to a Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d3236b2-a592-5826-8dbc-6e0f05ca23e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d3236b2-a592-5826-8dbc-6e0f05ca23e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429841} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2b163e-f418-5803-9223-687190c52195" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b868e1e-2207-586e-9bc6-bd4ad015584e" :question/math-academy-id "q-172531" :question/problem "${0}^{4}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, ${0}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $0$ and the exponent is $4.$\n\nThis means that $0$ is taken as a factor $4$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{0}^{4}=\\underbrace{0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0}_{4\\text{ times}}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b58f922-9f55-544a-970e-5a4e821130fe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Dimension"}] :question/id #uuid "24d4fbd9-9a1b-5736-ae95-b5cb844d887d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2160" :question/problem "Denise sends a package to her father. The total volume of the package is $75$ cubic inches, and it is $6$ inches wide and $2.5$ inches high. By modeling the package as a rectangular prism, what is the length of the package according to this model?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the volume of a rectangular package can be calculated as\n\n$$\nV = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nand we are told that this volume is equal to $75 \\, \\text{in}^3$.\n\n![](images/32/32798500f85c9bc6dfc97b588d787ec9befd8a4f5b52aa0bc482588f71ca989f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n$75 = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\times 6 \\times 2.5$\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n6 \\times 2.5 = 15\n$$\n\n, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot \\underbrace{6 \\cdot 2.5}_{15} \\\\\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the relationship between multiplication and division, we obtain that the length of the package must be\n$\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} = 75 \\div 15 = 5 \\, \\text{in}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"}] :question/id #uuid "830169cc-f331-5a91-81d6-374aad29e951" :question/math-academy-id "e-13761" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac{12}{7}\\times \\dfrac{21}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}$:\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{4}\\times\\dfrac{21}{7}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\frac{12}{4}}_{3}\\times \\underbrace{\\frac{21}{7}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147171 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40344731-b251-5ba9-8e05-775144455c95" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "a4cc2406-5f53-5a80-9e85-9cafd1fa2576" :question/math-academy-id "e-14182" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$." :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\underbrace{\\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}}_{{{\\color{blue}{2}}}\\text{ times}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}; = \\frac{4 \\cdot 4}{5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{16}{25}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{16}{25}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147172 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3cbc34d-4bd5-5b12-81a4-81d0c75c4780" :knowledge-point/title "Cubing a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "24da41ad-50c6-5654-8ebe-4c47c51ebd5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14184" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3 =$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\underbrace{\\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}}_{{{\\color{blue}{3}}}\\text{ times}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{9 \\cdot 3}{25 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{27}{125}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{27}{125}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147183 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147412 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc0f11b9-07e3-5659-ab34-4ad1c30310d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating One-Digit Decimal Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "027603b5-e3f3-55a1-836b-d41fb555bfec" :question/math-academy-id "e-14479" :question/problem "Evaluate $(0.5)^3$." :question/worked-solution "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.5)^{3} &= \\underbrace{0.5 \\cdot 0.5 \\cdot 0.5}_{{{\\color{blue}{3}}}\\text{ times}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute this product, we first ignore any decimal points and multiply the factors just as we would with whole numbers. We also ignore any leading zeros during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{5 \\times 5 \\times 5}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}3}} \\text{ times}}} = 125\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}3$ factors.\n\nThere is ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ decimal place in $0.5$.\nTherefore, the exponent $(0.5)^{\\color{blue}3}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{1}}\\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = \\color{red}3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $125$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{125}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}3}} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(0.5)^3 = 0.125\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147184 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7701af1-73f4-5da0-a029-1737dfbec48d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Two-Digit Decimal Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "597f5dfa-9e92-58c0-a3dd-f8205a382dc2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14480" :question/problem "Find the value of $(4.2)^2$." :question/worked-solution "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4.2)^{2} &= \\underbrace{4.2 \\cdot 4.2}_{{{\\color{blue}{2}}}\\text{ times}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{ccccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{lightgray}\\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}}\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{